choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Bottle, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Bottle)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Bottle
TEXT:

"Uncle Enrique," Suzie chokes out through her tears. "It's Rose. She..."

She clutches his collar, her voice breaking into helpless gasps.

"She's dead, Uncle. Rose is dead."

She throws her arms around his neck, burying her face against his shoulder.

"Rose is... dead?" Enrique repeats, the warmth instantly draining from his voice.

His expression hardens. He raises his head, his dark eyes locking onto our group with sudden, cold intensity.

"Are they the ones responsible?" he asks, his hand dropping to the pommel of his gem-studded sword as the blade clears the scabbard with a sharp hiss.

"No!" Suzie cries, pulling at his shirt. "It wasn't them! They fought to protect her! They are her companions!"

"I am the one who failed her," Daren says, stepping between Enrique and the rest of us. He bows his head low before the man. "I gave my word to keep her from harm, and I failed. No apology I offer can ever suffice..."

Enrique studies the misery etched across Daren's face. The tension slowly leaves his frame; he slides the sword back into its sheath with a dull click, crosses the distance, and rests a firm hand on Daren's pauldron.

"No apologies, my friend," Enrique says quietly. "It is I who must apologize for drawing steel so hastily. You carry the weight of this as heavily as any man could."

He steps past Daren, scanning each of us in turn, and draws himself upright.

"Take heart, friends!" he says, his voice carrying across the courtyard. "Why wear such grim expressions? Rose would despise seeing you in mourning. She would demand a smile. We do not honor her by brooding over what is lost; we celebrate your safe return. As Rose's companions, my home is yours."

He turns back to Suzie.

"Suzie! Fetch the vintage from the lower cellar—the reserve we set aside for special occasions. You know the cask, yes?"

"Yes, Uncle," Suzie says, scrubbing her cheeks with her cuffs. The weeping ceases as purpose takes over. "I will fetch it at once."

"Now then, companions of Rose," Enrique says as the girl hurries toward the front doors. "Before we raise a glass, give me your names. It is unseemly to drink with strangers."

choice(""Is this really the time and place to have a drink?"", B2-Ch01a-Time, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Time)
choice(""Shouldn't we be discussing what we're going to do with the kids, first?"", B2-Ch01a-Discussing, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Discussing)
choice(""If we're going to have a drink, then why not go all in and have some dwarven ale?"", B2-Ch01a-Dwarven, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Dwarven)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Time
TEXT:

"Is this really the time and place to have a drink?"

"What are you saying?" Enrique asks with an expansive wave of his hand. "Wine is always fitting. It draws people together, cuts through sorrow, and binds strangers into comrades. Ask your dwarven companion. Dwarves are famous for drinking until the casks run dry, yet no folk in the world stand more united."

"Aye," Hadrik grunts. "Though around the Northern taverns they say we only stay united because we're too drunk to remember who we were furious with yesterday. I'm Hadrik, by the way. Good to meet you."

Hadrik takes Enrique's hand in a crushing grip, then gestures down our line.

"This here is Barry. The armored wall is Daren. Over there is Kate, and the young one is Flower. We also travel with two banshees—one sharing the girl's body named Illuna, and the one locked in this amulet named Arraka."

A faint, muffled rattle drifts from the closed amulet as Hadrik finishes, but no one pays it any mind.

"Two banshees?" Enrique's eyebrows shoot up. "And Rose counted both as friends?"

"Hardly both," Hadrik chuckles. "The one in the gold is a prisoner who would happily flay us alive. But Illuna and Rose were dear companions."

"In that case, she is welcome under my roof, spirit or no," Enrique says. "Ah, here is our vintage!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Ella, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Ella) if (v_ch11_saved_slave != 3)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-No-Ella, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-No-Ella) if (v_ch11_saved_slave == 3)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Discussing
TEXT:

"Shouldn't we be discussing what we're going to do with the kids, first?"

"You need not worry about the children," Enrique says, waving off the concern. "I have matters well in hand."

"In what way?" I ask.

"All details will be laid out once the glasses are filled," Enrique replies with a grin. "Weighty decisions demand the clarity of fine wine. Your dwarven friend will surely back me on that."

"If you asked me, I'd swap the fine wine for a draught of Northern ale," Hadrik says. "But I won't argue with the principle. I'm Hadrik, by the way. Good to meet you."

Hadrik takes Enrique's hand in a crushing grip, then gestures down our line.

"This here is Barry. The armored wall is Daren. Over there is Kate, and the young one is Flower. We also travel with two banshees—one sharing the girl's body named Illuna, and the one locked in this amulet named Arraka."

A faint, muffled rattle drifts from the closed amulet as Hadrik finishes, but no one pays it any mind.

"Two banshees?" Enrique's eyebrows shoot up. "And Rose counted both as friends?"

"Hardly both," Hadrik chuckles. "The one in the gold is a prisoner who would happily flay us alive. But Illuna and Rose were dear companions."

"In that case, she is welcome under my roof, spirit or no," Enrique says. "Ah, here is our vintage!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Ella, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Ella) if (v_ch11_saved_slave != 3)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-No-Ella, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-No-Ella) if (v_ch11_saved_slave == 3)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Dwarven
TEXT:

"If we're going to have a drink, then why not go all in and have some dwarven ale?" I say. "We have some extra bottles of it stashed up in our backpacks."

"Ah, dwarven ale!" Enrique laughs heartily. "It has been far too long since I tasted proper malt. But I assure you, the reserve Suzie is bringing will make dwarven brew taste like puddle water by comparison."

"Now that is a bold claim," Hadrik says, eyes narrowing in challenge. "You've set the bar high, friend. I'm Hadrik, by the way. Good to meet you."

Hadrik takes Enrique's hand in a crushing grip, then gestures down our line.

"This here is Barry. The armored wall is Daren. Over there is Kate, and the young one is Flower. We also travel with two banshees—one sharing the girl's body named Illuna, and the one locked in this amulet named Arraka."

A faint, muffled rattle drifts from the closed amulet as Hadrik finishes, but no one pays it any mind.

"Two banshees?" Enrique's eyebrows shoot up. "And Rose counted both as friends?"

"Hardly both," Hadrik chuckles. "The one in the gold is a prisoner who would happily flay us alive. But Illuna and Rose were dear companions."

"In that case, she is welcome under my roof, spirit or no," Enrique says. "Ah, here is our vintage!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Ella, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Ella) if (v_ch11_saved_slave != 3)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-No-Ella, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-No-Ella) if (v_ch11_saved_slave == 3)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Ella
TEXT:

Suzie hurries back across the lawn, cradling a dusty dark bottle in both hands. Behind her comes Ella, walking with steady, quiet steps, carrying a polished tray set with crystal goblets. She looks fully recovered from the previous night's ordeal.

The two girls set the tray and bottle upon the wooden table under the arbor, and Suzie works the cork free with practiced hands.

"By the heavens," Enrique breathes, his gaze landing on Ella. "My eyes are blessed by such grace."

He bows with practiced flair, takes Ella's fingers, and brushes his moustache against the back of her hand.

"Were my heart not pledged to Rose for all eternity, I would have fallen on the spot."

Ella blinks, uncertain what to make of the display, then offers a formal, modest curtsy.

"My name is Ella," she says softly. "Suzie told me of Miss Rose. I am deeply sorry for your loss."

A shadow passes over Enrique's features, but he forces the bright, easy smile back into place before anyone can dwell on it.

"No apologies needed, my dear," he says. "You bear no blame in this."

"I take it you are the master of this estate now," Ella says, lowering her eyes. "I owe you an account of myself. Miss Rose took me in only yesterday to serve as..." She hesitates, her voice dropping to a whisper. "As a slave. I am trained in household duties, cooking, and mending, and I can read and write. If you permit me to remain, I will serve you as faithfully as I served her."

"Stop right there, Ella," Enrique says gently, his fingers tipping her chin upward until her eyes meet his. "No more of that talk. The Rose I knew would never keep a slave under her roof. Whatever you endured before, when you crossed that threshold, you entered as a free woman. So long as you dwell in this house, you are forbidden from using that word. Do we understand each other?"

"Yes, sir," Ella murmurs, blinking through a sudden sheen of tears, a faint smile touching her lips.

"Splendid!" Enrique turns to the rest of us, clapping his hands together. "Well? The vintage will not drink itself!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Ella2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Ella2)


ID: B2-Ch01a-No-Ella
TEXT:

Suzie returns across the lawn, carrying the dark glass bottle in one hand and balancing a tray of goblets in the other.

"Careful with those," Enrique says, stepping forward to relieve her of the tray. "Good girl."

They set the tray and bottle onto the wooden table under the arbor. Suzie sets to work on the cork, while Enrique looks back at us with an expectant grin.

"Well? The vintage will not drink itself!"

"Uncle," Kevin says, tugging at Enrique's unlaced shirt. His eyes are red and swollen, his voice trembling. "Uncle, what will become of us? How are we supposed to live without Rose? We tried to learn. We managed on our own while she traveled—we bought food, we cooked over the hearth, we kept the fire burning—but we can't... we can't—"

"Hush now, my boy," Enrique says softly, resting a heavy hand atop Kevin's head and ruffling his hair. "You will not be living alone. Starting tomorrow, I am moving back into the estate."

Kevin looks up, sniffing. "You... you are?"

"Of course I am," Enrique says with an easy laugh. "Would I lie to you? As soon as my trunks are hauled over, I will be right here, just like old times."

Suzie drops the cork on the table and throws her arms around her uncle's waist.

"Thank you, Uncle!" she cries. "Thank you! I thought you would never come back!"

"Forgive the interruption," Kate cuts in, her arms crossed over her chest. "Rose explained that you parted ways because you wished to pursue other women. Have you discussed this arrangement with your current wife?"

"Lover," Enrique corrects smoothly.

Kate's brow arches. "What?"

"Lover, not wife," Enrique says. "Rose was my wife. She remains my only wife."

"Fine. Your lover," Kate says, unimpressed. "How do you expect her to react? Will she gladly move into this estate to raise another woman's siblings?"

"Heavens, no!" Enrique laughs. "She will be absolutely furious. She despises children."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Body, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Body)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Ella2
TEXT:

"Uncle," Kevin says, tugging at Enrique's unlaced shirt. His eyes are red and swollen, his voice trembling. "Uncle, what will become of us? How are we supposed to live without Rose? We tried to learn. We managed on our own while she traveled—we bought food, we cooked over the hearth, we kept the fire burning—but we can't... we can't—"

"Hush now, my boy," Enrique says softly, resting a heavy hand atop Kevin's head and ruffling his hair. "You will not be living alone. Starting tomorrow, I am moving back into the estate."

Kevin looks up, sniffing. "You... you are?"

"Of course I am," Enrique says with an easy laugh. "Would I lie to you? As soon as my trunks are hauled over, I will be right here, just like old times."

Suzie drops the cork on the table and throws her arms around her uncle's waist.

"Thank you, Uncle!" she cries. "Thank you! I thought you would never come back!"

