"Heavens, no!" Enrique laughs. "She will be absolutely furious. She despises children."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Body, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Body)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Body
TEXT:

"Then how can you promise a home on her behalf without asking first?" Kate asks.

"I never promised on her behalf," Enrique says. "I intend to end the relationship the moment I return home today."

"You are ending things with your lover... just to care for Rose's siblings?" Kate stares at him, caught off guard.

"Naturally!" Enrique beams. "Why look so stunned? I will miss her company, certainly, but she only fancied me for my coin and my profile. I will find another without much trouble. The world is full of charming women."

He chuckles, entirely unbothered, and lifts a filled goblet from the table.

"Now then, take up your glasses, everyone. A toast to Rose: the most generous, radiant soul I have ever known, and the true love of my life. May her memory endure, and may she dwell in our hearts forevermore."

We lift our glasses and drink.

The next half hour passes in shared memories of Rose. We recount our days on the trail, and Enrique speaks of their years in the estate. None of us have an appetite for wine, but that hardly matters. Enrique is right—Rose would hate to leave us drowning in remorse, and avoiding her name out of grief would dishonor her memory.

Beneath the theatrical bravado, Enrique carries the loss harder than he lets on. Yet whenever he speaks of Rose, his smile softens into genuine affection, and even Kate lets out a quiet laugh once or twice as the stories unfold.

With our deadline creeping closer, we excuse ourselves to collect our packs from the guest quarters. As we shoulder our gear in the courtyard, Enrique gestures us aside, away from the children.

"There is one matter I could not raise before Kevin and Suzie," Enrique says quietly, the easy smile gone. "You returned without Rose's body. I drew my own conclusions, but I must ask. How did she die?"

choice("Tell him about the revenant.", B2-Ch01a-Circumstances, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Circumstances, v_b2_ch1_truth = 1)
choice("Tell him that Rose's body was incinerated by a fire elementalist mage.", B2-Ch01a-Circumstances, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Circumstances, v_b2_ch1_truth = 0)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Circumstances
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch1_truth == 1) {
"The truth is difficult to accept," I say.

"I am no stranger to the arcane," Enrique replies. "Speak plainly."

"After Rose was struck down, her body was... claimed," I say. "By an entity called a revenant."

"Claimed?" Enrique's brow knits.

"A dark spirit seized her corpse the moment her soul departed," Daren cuts in.

"And there is no spell, no ritual, to restore her if you destroy this creature?" Enrique asks.

"None," Daren says, looking him in the eye. "I am sorry."

Enrique exhales slowly through his nose. "I understand." He looks toward the house where the children are sitting. "If Kevin or Suzie ask, I will tell them her body was consumed by fire. I would rather spare them nightmares."

"We will hold to the same story if they ever ask us," Daren says.

"Thank you." Enrique turns to face our group. "Companions of Rose, I regret that we met under such dark skies. Know this: whether you linger in Thilias or depart by dusk, my doors stand open to you always. Walk with the gods."

"May they watch over us all," Daren answers. "Farewell, Enrique."

We bid our farewells to the children and head out, taking the most direct route toward the citadel. Signposts stand at nearly every crossing in this quarter, pointing the way to the palace gates.

"Do you think they will be safe?" Kate asks in a low voice as we navigate the paved alleys. "With Enrique, I mean. I am not certain how much faith to put in that man."

"Rose trusted him to look after them while she traveled," I say. "His affection for the kids is real enough, and they clearly adore him. They'll be fine."

"I suppose," Kate says, staring ahead. "He did not hesitate when he spoke of breaking things off with his lover. Perhaps I am looking for trouble where there is none."

"Look, another marker," Hadrik points ahead. "Still on course."

The march to the royal citadel takes just under two hours. At the outer barricades, Daren presents our names to the guard captain. After a brief inspection, the iron portcullis grinds upward. We file into the courtyard, and the heavy gates crash shut behind us.

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_truth == 0) {
"Her body was consumed by fire magic shortly after the ambush," I say.

"I see," Enrique says, his shoulders sinking. "I feared as much. I will avoid the topic with the children, but if they press me, I will give them your account."

He draws himself upright and faces our group.

"Companions of Rose, I regret that we met under such dark skies. Know this: whether you linger in Thilias or depart by dusk, my doors stand open to you always. Walk with the gods."

"May they watch over us all," Daren answers. "Farewell, Enrique."

We bid our farewells to the children and head out, taking the most direct route toward the citadel. Signposts stand at nearly every crossing in this quarter, pointing the way to the palace gates.

"I wonder if lying to him was the right choice," Kate says as we walk. "He was her ex-husband. He deserved the full truth."

"Enrique has spent his life sheltered behind these walls, Kate," Daren says. "Even seasoned warriors struggle to stomach walking corpses. Burdening a civilian with the horror of a revenant would give him nightmares for decades without changing the outcome."

"Perhaps you are right," Kate concedes quietly. "I cannot judge how the truth would have weighed on him."

"Look, another marker," Hadrik points ahead. "Still on course."

The march to the royal citadel takes just under two hours. At the outer barricades, Daren presents our names to the guard captain. After a brief inspection, the iron portcullis grinds upward. We file into the courtyard, and the heavy gates crash shut behind us.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Hallway, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Hallway)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Lover
TEXT:

When we reach the estate, Kevin and Suzie run out across the courtyard to throw their arms around Rose. We leave her to catch up with the children while we head up to the guest rooms to collect our packs. By the time we return to the courtyard, an unfamiliar figure stands on the gravel beside Rose and the kids.

He stands nearly as tall as Daren, lean and athletic, with an unlaced linen shirt baring half his chest. Dark hair falls past his collar, and a thick, well-kept moustache curls over his upper lip. At his hip rests a gilded scabbard gleaming with rows of polished gemstones. He pauses in his conversation with Rose as we approach, studying our group with keen, dark eyes.

"Ah, you have acquired quite the retinue of tournament mages on your travels," the man says to Rose, smiling warmly. "Will you introduce me to your companions?"

"Of course," Rose says, turning to us. "Everyone, this is my ex-husband, Enrique. He was just giving me an account of the house while I was away."

She gestures down our line.

"Enrique, these are the friends who brought me home safely and pulled me from harm more times than I can count. From right to left: Flower, Illuna, Kate, Barry, Daren, and Hadrik."

"Hold on," Enrique blinks, glancing between Flower and Rose. "You gave two names for the young lady."

"It is... a bit intricate," Rose says, blushing slightly. "Flower is the girl, and Illuna is an elven spirit sharing her body. They coexist peacefully."

"A girl and a banshee sharing a single vessel?" Enrique's eyebrows lift in genuine admiration. "Now there is a marvel you do not see every day."

He steps up to Daren and catches his hand in a hearty grip.

"You must be Rose's new suitor!" Enrique beams. "Look after her well, my friend. She is an extraordinary woman."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Unfair, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Unfair) if (v_ch11_romance_rose == 0)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Offend, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Offend) if (v_ch11_romance_rose == 1)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Unfair
TEXT:

"Actually, I am not—" Daren begins, but Rose cuts in before he can finish.

"Enrique," she says, her fingers gripping his forearm with surprising force. "Do you remember what occurred the last time you assumed a visitor was my lover?"

"Rose, you are being entirely unreasonable!" Enrique protests. "How was I to know the woman beside him was his wife? They shared such similar features I took her for his sister. Anyone could have made the error! And notice that this time, I ensured no woman was standing beside the gentleman before I spoke."

Rose lets out a quiet sigh and releases his arm.

"Come, Daren, we must move," she says. "The king will not wait on us."

"Ah, the decree from the screens!" Enrique says, clapping his hands together. "Golmyck was speaking of your company in that closing summons?"

"He was," Rose nods. "I have to guide them to the citadel, so I will be gone for a few hours. Will you remain with Kevin and Suzie until I return?"

"Naturally!" Enrique says. "That is why I came."

"Thank you, Enrique," Rose says warmly. "We will speak more when I return. Take care."

"Farewell, sweet Rose!" Enrique calls out as we step into the lane. "And safe travels to all of you. We shall share tales over a proper vintage when you return!"

We bid our farewells to Enrique and the children and set off through the upper quarter. Rose takes the lead, pointing out decorative fountains, distinct archways, and painted signposts along the way so we can navigate back to the mansion on our own later.

"I apologize for that," Rose says softly to Daren as we turn down a broad cobblestone lane. "Enrique worries that I have not taken another partner since our separation. He tends to get carried away."

"Think nothing of it," Daren says with a dry chuckle. "Though I admit, I feel for the fellow who arrived with his wife. Were you able to clear that misunderstanding?"

"I tried," Rose admits, wincing. "I explained everything to her, but she looked rather unconvinced. They are still married, but she has not spoken a word to me since that afternoon."

"I cannot blame her," Daren says. "I suspect even my wife would look at you with a critical eye after an introduction like that."

choice(""You're married?"", B2-Ch01a-Married, v_ac_b2_ch1_distance = 1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Married)
choice(""Wait a minute. I'm pretty sure I read somewhere that you were never married."", B2-Ch01a-Somewhere, v_ac_b2_ch1_distance = 1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Somewhere)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Offend
TEXT:

"Actually, I am not—" Daren begins, but Hadrik cuts him off with a booming laugh.

"Wrong man!" Hadrik grins, clapping a heavy hand on my shoulder. "Barry is the one you should be congratulating, not Daren!"

"Him?" Enrique turns his gaze to me, blinking in surprise.

He recovers instantly, stepping forward to clasp my shoulder with theatrical warmth.

"My apologies, friend! I should know better than to judge by outward appearances alone. If Rose has granted you her favor, I trust her instincts implicitly. I wish you both long years of joy together!"

"All right, all right, you have given your blessing!" Rose's face burns crimson as she grabs my sleeve and drags me toward the gate. "Barry needs to reach the castle before the deadline. The king of Thilias is waiting."

"Ah, the decree from the screens!" Enrique says, clapping his hands together. "Golmyck was speaking of your company in that closing summons?"

"He was," Rose nods. "I have to guide them to the citadel, so I will be gone for a few hours. Will you remain with Kevin and Suzie until I return?"

"Naturally!" Enrique says. "That is why I came."

"Thank you, Enrique," Rose says warmly. "We will speak more when I return. Take care."

"Farewell, sweet Rose!" Enrique calls out as we step into the lane. "And safe travels to all of you. We shall share tales over a proper vintage when you return!"

We bid our farewells to Enrique and the children and set off through the upper quarter. Rose takes the lead, pointing out decorative fountains, distinct archways, and painted signposts along the way so we can navigate back to the mansion on our own later.

"I am sorry about that," Rose murmurs, falling into step beside me. "Enrique worries that I haven't been with anyone since we parted, and that was his way of showing he holds no resentment toward you."

"I figured as much," I say. "Don't worry about it."

Rose gives me a small smile. She glances over her shoulder to confirm the others are out of earshot, then drops her voice to a whisper.

"I should also apologize for slipping away early this morning without saying goodbye. I did not want to make a spectacle of what happened between us. You saw how the others reacted when Flower blurted it out... And with the tournament underway, our paths will diverge soon. We may not even cross paths again once you leave Thilias."

choice(""Hey, now, don't go writing me off before the tournament's even started!"", B2-Ch01a-Writing, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Writing)
choice(""Actually, I was thinking that we could keep in contact by using Daren's transceivers."", B2-Ch01a-Transceivers, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Transceivers)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Married
TEXT:

achievement("Long-distance",v_ac_b2_ch1_distance)
"You're married?"

"In a manner of speaking," Daren says, adjusting the strap of his breastplate.

"How can someone be married 'in a manner of speaking'?" I ask.

"Sophie remains in our home village on the Southern Continent," Daren explains. "When I took to the road to offer my healing where it was needed, I asked for a divorce to spare her the danger that follows a traveling warrior. She refused to hear of it. In the end, I offered a compromise—she was free to take another husband if she wished, regardless of our vows."

"Did she?" Rose asks softly.

"Never," Daren says, a quiet fondness softening his rough features. "I tried to push her away, convincing myself it was for her protection. I would vanish for years without sending a single letter, yet whenever I returned, she was waiting. The villagers knew us both and kept our marriage a secret from outsiders. When I realized she would never let go, I stopped fighting it and focused on finding ways to bridge the distance. That was when I began enchanting transceivers to extend their range. The pair I carry allows us to speak across oceans."

"Can you reach her from here?" I ask.

"No," Daren sighs. "I tried the moment we arrived. Varathia is either beyond the spell's reach, or some enchantment over this island blocks communication with the outside world."

"Perhaps that is for the best," Kate says from behind us. "Telling her what you survived this past week would hardly bring her peace of mind. She is better off in the dark."

"Perhaps," Daren concedes quietly.

"Look, another signpost for the citadel," Rose says, pointing toward a stone archway ahead. "They appear more frequently as we near the palace gates. Follow me—down this lane next."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Entrance, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Entrance)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Somewhere
TEXT:

achievement("Long-distance",v_ac_b2_ch1_distance)
"Wait a minute. I'm pretty sure I read somewhere that you were never married."

"I deliberately spread false rumors to that effect," Daren admits. "I have made dangerous enemies over the years, and none of them would hesitate to use my wife as leverage against me."

"Where is she now?" Rose asks.

"Sophie remains in our home village on the Southern Continent," Daren explains. "When I took to the road to offer my healing where it was needed, I asked for a divorce to spare her the danger that follows a traveling warrior. She refused to hear of it. In the end, I offered a compromise—she was free to take another husband if she wished, regardless of our vows."

"Did she?" Rose asks softly.

"Never," Daren says, a quiet fondness softening his rough features. "I tried to push her away, convincing myself it was for her protection. I would vanish for years without sending a single letter, yet whenever I returned, she was waiting. The villagers knew us both and kept our marriage a secret from outsiders. When I realized she would never let go, I stopped fighting it and focused on finding ways to bridge the distance. That was when I began enchanting transceivers to extend their range. The pair I carry allows us to speak across oceans."

"Can you reach her from here?" I ask.

"No," Daren sighs. "I tried the moment we arrived. Varathia is either beyond the spell's reach, or some enchantment over this island blocks communication with the outside world."

"Perhaps that is for the best," Kate says from behind us. "Telling her what you survived this past week would hardly bring her peace of mind. She is better off in the dark."

"Perhaps," Daren concedes quietly.

"Look, another signpost for the citadel," Rose says, pointing toward a stone archway ahead. "They appear more frequently as we near the palace gates. Follow me—down this lane next."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Entrance, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Entrance)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Entrance
TEXT:

The walk to the royal citadel takes the better part of two hours. Having arrived with ample time before our scheduled audience, we linger near the outer courtyard to bid Rose farewell.

"All right, all right," Rose laughs as Kate finally releases her from a fierce embrace. "Enough with the long faces. It is only an audience with the king. It is not as if we are parting forever."

"Keep that transceiver on you at all times," Daren reminds her, his brow furrowed. "If things take a dark turn and we are detained, we will send word."

"Daren and his tireless optimism," Rose smiles, shaking her head. "You will be fine. Golmyck requested a conversation, not an arrest. Otherwise we would have met royal marshals on our doorstep, not an invitation."

"Let us hope your reading holds," Daren mutters.

"The main archway is straight ahead," Rose says, stepping back toward the avenue. "I have kept you lingering long enough. While you settle matters with the crown, I am preparing a supper that will put Flower's hearth-magic to shame, so do not dare be late."

"Wouldn't miss it for the world," I say.

Rose waves, turning back down the sunlit boulevard. We turn toward the massive iron-banded gates of the inner ward. Daren signals the sentries on the parapet; after a sharp exchange of names, the counterweights groan and the heavy portcullis lifts. We file inside, the iron teeth crashing into the stone behind us with a resonant thud.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Hallway, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Hallway)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Writing
TEXT:

"Hey, now, don't go writing me off before the tournament's even started!" I say. "There have been regular people who won contests against mages before, you know."

Rose lets out a quiet laugh.

"Yes, and every recorded instance was an ale-drinking match," she says. "You understand my meaning, Barry... Even if you triumph in the arena, we belong to two entirely different worlds. Continents isolated from one another for six hundred years."

"So what?" I say. "The champion earns the right to enter and leave Varathia whenever he pleases. Just wait for me. The moment I claim the Magium and secure my powers, I'm coming straight back. That's a promise."

A warm flush creeps up Rose's neck, coloring her cheeks.

"Hey, Rose!" Hadrik's booming voice cuts through the quiet as he and the others close the distance behind us. "Are these carved stone pillars the markers we should watch for on the return trek?"

He pulls up short, catching sight of Rose's crimson face, and glances between us with an exaggerated arch of his brow.

"Am I... interrupting something?" he asks, grinning broadly.

"Not in the least!" Rose stammers, smoothing her skirt in a flurry of motion. "Yes, those are precisely the landmarks you need. They grow more frequent the closer we get to the gates. Come along, down this alleyway next!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Entrance, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Entrance)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Transceivers
TEXT:

"Actually, I was thinking that we could keep in contact by using Daren's transceivers," I say. "He told me that he's enchanted them to cover a much longer range than regular transceivers, so there shouldn't be any problems."

"You understand my meaning, Barry..." Rose says softly. "Even if we exchange words across the distance, we belong to two entirely different worlds. Continents isolated from one another for six hundred years."

"So what?" I say. "The champion earns the right to enter and leave Varathia whenever he pleases. Just wait for me. The moment I claim the Magium and secure my powers, I'm coming straight back. That's a promise."

A warm flush creeps up Rose's neck, coloring her cheeks.

"Hey, Rose!" Hadrik's booming voice cuts through the quiet as he and the others close the distance behind us. "Are these carved stone pillars the markers we should watch for on the return trek?"

He pulls up short, catching sight of Rose's crimson face, and glances between us with an exaggerated arch of his brow.

"Am I... interrupting something?" he asks, grinning broadly.

"Not in the least!" Rose stammers, smoothing her skirt in a flurry of motion. "Yes, those are precisely the landmarks you need. They grow more frequent the closer we get to the gates. Come along, down this alleyway next!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Entrance, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Entrance)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Hallway
TEXT:

The citadel feels wrong. The impression took hold before we even crossed the threshold—every high window frame has empty sockets where decorative jewels were roughly chiseled away, and the stone lintels along the battlements show jagged scars where ornaments were torn free.

Inside, the austerity is worse. The corridors stand stripped to bare stone. No tapestries line the masonry, no rugs cover the flagstones, and no gilded urns rest on the alcoves. The corridor feels more like a prison gallery than a royal seat. Not a single guardsman patrols the hall. For a second, I wonder if we took a wrong turn into an abandoned wing.

"Feels like the ogres' stronghold all over again," Hadrik grunts, clicking open Arraka's amulet. "Hey! You in the box! Are there invisible sentries lurking around us? Speak up!"

The amulet remains dead silent, the banshee refusing to offer so much as an insult.

"Giving me the quiet treatment, eh?" Hadrik smirks.

Without a word of warning, Kate drops her hand, and a wave of frost rushes outward along the floor and walls, encasing the entire hallway in thick, gleaming rime.

"The passage is clear," Kate says, her breath misting in the sudden cold. "No one is hiding nearby."

"Wonderful," Daren snaps. "Now thaw the damn stone before someone sees—"

A wooden door at the far end creaks open, and a man in a plain servant's tunic pokes his head into the passage.

"His Majesty will—" The servant halts mid-sentence, his eyes going wide as he takes in the frozen hall, icicles hanging from the sconces.

He blinks twice, clears his throat, and continues in a flat, polite monotone as if frostbitten corridors were a daily occurrence.

"His Majesty will receive you shortly. Please remain in this corridor until your audience commences."

He pulls the door shut with a soft click.

"Well, you heard the man!" Flower beams. "We have time to kill. Let's go exploring!"

"We will do nothing of the sort," Daren says, exasperated. "He told us to wait right here. And Kate needs to clear this ice before the king walks in."

"I cannot undo it," Kate says flatly. "It will melt on its own in a few hours."

"We do not have a few hours!" Daren's voice rises. "If the king sees his palace turned into an ice cave, he'll throw us into the dungeons!"

"Relax," Hadrik chuckles. "What's the worst that could happen?"

"Good gods, man!" Daren throws his hands in the air. "Is there anything in this world that rattles you?"

A sudden sheet of bright crimson flame erupts around Flower, sweeping down the corridor in a warm rush. The rime flashes straight to steam with a sharp hiss, billowing toward the ceiling and dispersing into dry air. The flames vanish an instant later, leaving the masonry bone-dry and untouched.

"There," Flower says, brushing a lock of red hair from her forehead. "All fixed. Now, who wants to see what's behind the walls?"

choice(""Sure, I'll go."", B2-Ch01a-Bricks, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Bricks, v_b2_ch1_puzzle2 = 1)
choice(""No, I'd rather stay here."", B2-Ch01a-Bricks, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Bricks, v_b2_ch1_puzzle2 = 0)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Bricks
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch1_puzzle2 == 1) {
"Sure, I'll go."

"Perfect!" Flower says, her face lighting up. "Follow me!"

"Oh, come now," Daren groans, shaking his head. "What are we supposed to tell the chamberlain if he returns and finds half the company gone?"

"Tell him we stepped away for a moment," I say. "Call me on the transceiver when he shows."

Daren gives an irritated grunt but lets it drop. I catch up with Flower, who has stopped before a stretch of masonry a dozen paces down the hall. She cocks her head, running her fingertips along the mortar, then begins tapping each brick with her knuckles.

"Looking for something specific?" I ask.

"Shh, I'm listening to the hollows!"

She works her way down a seam, taps twice on a dark granite block, and grips the edges with both hands. With a sharp tug, she works the stone free and sets it on the floor. Inside the cavity rests a small wooden lever.

"You really know your way around old masonry," I note.

"Of course!" Flower beams. "Petal and I have explored dozens of ancient ruins. It's practically our hobby."

"Less a hobby," Illuna's dry voice cuts in as Flower's eyes flash brilliant blue, "and more a weekly habit of courting violent death."

"Found the release!" Flower says, her eyes shifting back to warm green as she wrenches the lever downward.

Gears grind inside the wall, and the surrounding stone blocks slide inward, parting to reveal a narrow passage. We slip through the opening into a low, dust-choked chamber centered around a waist-high stone pedestal. Carved glyphs ring the upper rim, obscured by shadow.

Flower flicks her wrist, conjuring a floating ember of fire above the stone to illuminate the surface. The inscription is chiseled in clean Common script.

"What does it say?" Flower leans over the pedestal.

"It says: 'Place your palm upon this pedestal, if you take joy in uncovering secrets.'"

"Oh, I love secrets!" Flower exclaims, and immediately slaps her palm flat against the polished stone.

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_puzzle2 == 0) {
"No, I'd rather stay here."

"Suit yourself," Flower shrugs cheerfully. "If anyone changes their mind, follow the noise. Otherwise, I'm going alone."

"Hold on," Daren calls after her. "What are we supposed to tell the servant if the king calls for us?"

"Tell him I will be right back," Flower replies over her shoulder. "The king has no business with me anyway. I am not in the tournament."

"I suppose that's true..." Daren mutters as Flower halts before a stretch of masonry and begins scrutinizing the mortar.

She taps the bricks one by one with her knuckles, listening to the resonance.

"What is she looking for?" Daren asks me in a low voice.

"A hidden latch, most likely."

Sure enough, Flower works a dark block of stone free from the wall and reaches into the opening. Machinery clicks behind the masonry, and a section of the wall slides inward, revealing a dark gap that she slips through without hesitation.

"She certainly knows her way around secret doors," I say.

"We did find her running laps through trap-laden ruins when we met," Hadrik reminds us with a grin.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Bricks2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Bricks2)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Bricks2
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch1_second,1) if (v_b2_ch1_puzzle2 == 1)
achievement("Second piece of the puzzle",v_ac_b2_ch1_second)
#if(v_b2_ch1_puzzle2 == 1 && v_ch8_first_puzzle == 1) {
A narrow slot opens at the base of the pedestal with a sharp click, ejecting two small items onto the stone before snapping shut. I scoop them up. One is a carved tile of dark stone—a puzzle fragment—and the other is a folded slip of parchment. I unfold the scrap under Flower's floating ember.

The ink is written in the same precise Common hand: "Gather every fragment of the design, and I will reveal my greatest work. I have hidden one within every structure I was commissioned to build."

It is signed simply: "The Architect."

The same craftsman who designed the forest ruins built this fortress. That makes two puzzle fragments in my pocket. I wonder how many structures the man designed across the continent.

"This is wonderful!" Flower says, bouncing on her heels. "I want one too!"

She plants her palm on the pedestal again. The stone remains cold and motionless.

"It won't give me another," she pouts. "Barry, you try!"

Daren's voice crackles from the transceiver at my belt. "Barry, do you copy?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_puzzle2 == 1 && v_ch8_first_puzzle != 1) {
A narrow slot opens at the base of the pedestal with a sharp click, ejecting two small items onto the stone before snapping shut. I scoop them up. One is a carved tile of dark stone—a puzzle fragment—and the other is a folded slip of parchment. I unfold the scrap under Flower's floating ember.

The ink is written in the same precise Common hand: "Gather every fragment of the design, and I will reveal my greatest work. I have hidden one within every structure I was commissioned to build."

It is signed simply: "The Architect."

"This is wonderful!" Flower says, bouncing on her heels. "I want one too!"

She plants her palm on the pedestal again. The stone remains cold and motionless.

"It won't give me another," she pouts. "Barry, you try!"

Daren's voice crackles from the transceiver at my belt. "Barry, do you copy?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_puzzle2 == 0) {
While Flower remains behind the wall, the heavy door at the end of the hall swings open again, and the servant re-emerges.

"Daren and his company?" the man announces. "His Majesty will receive you now."

He closes the door without waiting for an answer.

"Hold on a second," Hadrik rumbles, bristling. "Since when are we 'Daren's company'? I thought we were running a council here."

"It is a figure of speech," Kate says dryly. "What else would an outsider call us when Daren is the only name on the proclamation?"

"I don't care for it," Hadrik grunts. "They knew Kate was an artificial mage and Barry was lessathi. They could have managed the rest of our names. We wrote them down at the registry, after all."

"Flower!" Daren calls toward the secret passage, cutting Hadrik off. "Flower, wrap it up! The king has summoned us!"

"Coming!" Flower calls back. She darts through the opening, trips the latch inside the cavity, and fits the loose brick back into the wall with practiced speed.

"What kept you in there all this time?" Daren asks her.

"A secret," Flower says, flashing a bright grin.

"Of course," Daren sighs. "Come on, move."

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_puzzle2 == 1) {
"I hear you, Daren," I say, unhooking the transceiver with my right hand.

Flower scowls at being ignored, grabs my left wrist, and forces my palm down onto the pedestal. The mechanism clicks again, the base sliding open to spit out a second stone fragment and parchment. Flower scoops both up and stuffs them into her pocket with a satisfied grin.

"The chamberlain returned," Daren says through the device. "The king is ready to see us. Get back here at once."

"On our way," I reply.

We step back out into the main corridor. Flower resets the hidden lever, waits for the masonry to slide flush, and carefully seats the brick back into its socket.

"We wouldn't want anyone else claiming the prize before us, would we, Barry?" she whispers.

"I suppose not," I say as we jog back to rejoin the group.

"Well?" Daren asks when we reach them. "Did you find whatever you were after?"

"Yep!" Flower beams.

"Magnificent," Daren mutters. "Now let us face the king."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Reason, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Reason)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Reason
TEXT:

We push through the heavy doors and follow a vaulted stone corridor that opens into the royal audience chamber. Daren pauses at the final threshold, meeting each of our eyes to ensure we are braced, then leads the way inside.

The throne room is as austere as the rest of the citadel—bare granite walls, unadorned pillared arches, and a single strip of worn crimson carpet stretching toward the dais. On the raised platform sits Golmyck upon a plain stone throne, flanked by the servant on his right and an unfamiliar, sharply dressed nobleman on his left. The gnome has exchanged his workshop smock for formal regalia: a polished gold circlet sits atop his brow, and an ermine-trimmed crimson mantle drapes over his small shoulders. As we approach the dais, he offers an open, diplomatic smile.

"Welcome, travelers!" Golmyck greets us. "Make yourselves comfortable. Forgive the absence of chairs; court protocol insists that ordinary petitioners must remain standing in the presence of the crown."

"Dispense with the pleasantries, King of Thilias," the nobleman beside him snaps, adjusting the silver frogging on his coat. "This is neither the time nor the venue for idle court etiquette."

"Now, now, Ambassador," Golmyck replies mildly. "Manners preserve civil order, lest we slide into barbarism."

"You lecture me on barbarism while standing before self-confessed murderers?" the ambassador sneers, his face flushing red. "I did not ride from Ollendor to endure your homilies. I represent His Majesty King Alistair, and I demand that the killers of the king's royal brother answer for their crime!"

"Murderers they may be," Golmyck says evenly, "but they are also participants in the tournament and guests within my walls. Justice demands that I hear their account before pronouncing sentence."

He turns his gaze down the dais toward us.

"Speak, travelers," the gnome king says. "What justification do you offer for the slaying of the Earl of Ollendor?"

choice(""The guy was asking for it."", B2-Ch01a-Asking, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Asking)
choice(""We didn't kill him! We're innocent!"", B2-Ch01a-Innocent, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Innocent)
choice(""We killed him because he placed absolutely no value on human life."", B2-Ch01a-Value, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Value)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Asking
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch1_breed,1) if (v_b2_ch1_spin == 0)
achievement("Half-breed",v_ac_b2_ch1_breed)
#if(v_b2_ch1_spin == 0) {
"The guy was asking for it."

"You see?" the ambassador rounds on Golmyck, stabbing an accusatory finger toward me. "Do you see the caliber of ruffians you invite into your court?"

"Your Majesty," Daren cuts in, stepping between me and the dais with a deep bow. "Allow me to explain what transpired."

"Psst!" Arraka's rasp hisses from the amulet the moment Daren speaks. "Hey! Weakling. Stat-trickster. Half-lessathi. Over here!"

"Half... lessathi?" I mutter, edging closer to Hadrik's hip.

"Never mind that!" she whispers fiercely. "Trouble. Eiden just folded space into the middle of the hall. He's hovering right behind you under a concealment charm."

"Eiden?" I whisper back. "Why show himself now?"

"How should I know?" Arraka snarls. "I promised to warn you when that monster drew near, and I did. Now order this thick-skulled oaf to stop spinning me at every cobblestone!"

"Right, right," I say under my breath. "I'll see what I can do."

While we whisper, the dispute before the throne boils over.

"You would shield these cutthroats?!" the ambassador roars at Golmyck.

"I shield no one," Golmyck answers in a measured tone. "I merely state that law requires a fair hearing before sentence is passed."

"These dogs warrant no hearing!" the ambassador bellows, veins standing out on his neck. "They warrant summary execution! Do you seek war with Ollendor, King of Thilias? I speak with King Alistair's voice! I will not endure this farce when the guilt is plain. Put them to death this instant or face our legions! Do you hear me, Golmyck? War!"

"I am afraid I cannot permit you to execute these travelers, Ambassador," a soft voice drifts from the empty air behind us.

We spin around. Eiden materializes in the center of the crimson runner and strides forward, hands tucked casually into the pockets of his loose trousers. The ambassador blanches, beads of sweat breaking out across his forehead as his bluster evaporates.

"What is the matter, Ambassador?" Eiden asks, a lazy smirk tugging at his lips. "Won't you kneel before your Creator?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_spin == 1) {
"The guy was asking for it."

"You see?" the ambassador rounds on Golmyck, stabbing an accusatory finger toward me. "Do you see the caliber of ruffians you invite into your court?"

"Your Majesty," Daren cuts in, stepping between me and the dais with a deep bow. "Allow me to explain what transpired."

"I have heard quite enough explanations!" the ambassador sneers. "Your Majesty, these vagabonds are not worth squandering royal breath upon. Surrender them to my custody as I requested before this audience. I will see justice done, and Thilias will be rid of the matter."

"That is impossible, Ambassador," Golmyck says mildly. "While the victim hailed from Ollendor, the blood was spilled on the stones of Thilias, within my jurisdiction. Under the covenant signed six centuries ago by all crowns of Varathia, judgment resides with me, not with King Alistair."

"You would shield these cutthroats?!" the ambassador roars at the gnome king.

"I shield no one," Golmyck answers in a measured tone. "I merely state that law requires a fair hearing before sentence is passed."

"These dogs warrant no hearing!" the ambassador bellows, veins standing out on his neck. "They warrant summary execution! Do you seek war with Ollendor, King of Thilias? I speak with King Alistair's voice! I will not endure this farce when the guilt is plain. Put them to death this instant or face our legions! Do you hear me, Golmyck? War!"

"I am afraid I cannot permit you to execute these travelers, Ambassador," a soft voice drifts from the empty air behind us.

I whirl around, and the moment I spot Eiden's face, I snatch Arraka's amulet right out of Hadrik's hand.

"You!" I hiss at the gold casing. "You swore you would warn us whenever Eiden was lurking invisibly nearby!"

"Did I, now?" Arraka's muffled laughter echoes through the metal. "My recollection has grown terribly hazy of late. I wonder why... Oh, of course! It must be all the SPINNING!"

Eiden looks toward me, his eyes shut, an amused smile playing across his features. I realize too late that I just shouted at a piece of jewelry in a royal audience chamber. Without a word, he strolls past us toward the dais. The ambassador trembles, mopping his dripping brow with a silk kerchief.

"What is the matter, Ambassador?" Eiden asks, a lazy smirk tugging at his lips. "Won't you kneel before your Creator?"

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Creator, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Creator)


