"What is the matter, Ambassador?" Eiden asks, a lazy smirk tugging at his lips. "Won't you kneel before your Creator?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_spin == 1 && v_ch11_saved_slave == 3) {
"We didn't kill him! We're innocent!"

"Indeed?" Golmyck strokes his chin. "You ought to know by now how difficult it is to conceal information in my city. Are you certain you wish to open this hearing with a falsehood?"

"Your Majesty," Daren cuts in, stepping between me and the dais with a deep bow. "Allow me to explain what transpired."

"I have heard quite enough explanations!" the ambassador sneers. "Your Majesty, these vagabonds are not worth squandering royal breath upon. Surrender them to my custody as I requested before this audience. I will see justice done, and Thilias will be rid of the matter."

"That is impossible, Ambassador," Golmyck says mildly. "While the victim hailed from Ollendor, the blood was spilled on the stones of Thilias, within my jurisdiction. Under the covenant signed six centuries ago by all crowns of Varathia, judgment resides with me, not with King Alistair."

"You would shield these cutthroats?!" the ambassador roars at the gnome king.

"I shield no one," Golmyck answers in a measured tone. "I merely state that law requires a fair hearing before sentence is passed."

"These dogs warrant no hearing!" the ambassador bellows, veins standing out on his neck. "They warrant summary execution! Do you seek war with Ollendor, King of Thilias? I speak with King Alistair's voice! I will not endure this farce when the guilt is plain. Put them to death this instant or face our legions! Do you hear me, Golmyck? War!"

"I am afraid I cannot permit you to execute these travelers, Ambassador," a soft voice drifts from the empty air behind us.

I whirl around, and the moment I spot Eiden's face, I snatch Arraka's amulet right out of Hadrik's hand.

"You!" I hiss at the gold casing. "You swore you would warn us whenever Eiden was lurking invisibly nearby!"

"Did I, now?" Arraka's muffled laughter echoes through the metal. "My recollection has grown terribly hazy of late. I wonder why... Oh, of course! It must be all the SPINNING!"

Eiden looks toward me, his eyes shut, an amused smile playing across his features. I realize too late that I just shouted at a piece of jewelry in a royal audience chamber. Without a word, he strolls past us toward the dais. The ambassador trembles, mopping his dripping brow with a silk kerchief.

"What is the matter, Ambassador?" Eiden asks, a lazy smirk tugging at his lips. "Won't you kneel before your Creator?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_spin == 0 && v_ch11_kill_noble == 0 && v_ch11_saved_slave != 3) {
"We didn't kill him! We're innocent!"

"Innocent, you say?" Golmyck strokes his beard. "Our reports noted that a third party was present during the altercation in the street. Are you claiming this other individual struck the fatal blow?"

"Yes, exactly!" I say. "Eiden was the one who killed him, not us!"

"And who might this Eiden be?" Golmyck asks.

At the mention of the name, a high squeak escapes the ambassador's throat. His bluster vanishes instantly, his face draining of blood as sweat beads along his hairline.

"Is something amiss, Ambassador?" Golmyck asks, glancing over.

"Lies!" the ambassador gasps, his voice trembling. "The boy lies! That being would never deign to walk among such filth!"

"What being?" Golmyck asks. "Are you familiar with this Eiden?"

"Your Majesty," Daren cuts in, stepping between me and the dais with a deep bow. "Allow me to explain what transpired."

"Psst!" Arraka's rasp hisses from the amulet the moment Daren speaks. "Hey! Weakling. Stat-trickster. Half-lessathi. Over here!"

"Half... lessathi?" I mutter, edging closer to Hadrik's hip.

"Never mind that!" she whispers fiercely. "Trouble. Eiden just folded space into the middle of the hall. He's hovering right behind you under a concealment charm."

"Eiden?" I whisper back. "Why show himself now?"

"How should I know?" Arraka snarls. "I promised to warn you when that monster drew near, and I did. Now order this thick-skulled oaf to stop spinning me at every cobblestone!"

"Right, right," I say under my breath. "I'll see what I can do."

While we whisper, the dispute before the throne boils over.

"You would shield these cutthroats?!" the ambassador roars at Golmyck.

"I shield no one," Golmyck answers in a measured tone. "I merely state that law requires a fair hearing before sentence is passed."

"These dogs warrant no hearing!" the ambassador bellows, veins standing out on his neck. "They warrant summary execution! Do you seek war with Ollendor, King of Thilias? I speak with King Alistair's voice! I will not endure this farce when the guilt is plain. Put them to death this instant or face our legions! Do you hear me, Golmyck? War!"

"I am afraid I cannot permit you to execute these travelers, Ambassador," a soft voice drifts from the empty air behind us.

We spin around. Eiden materializes in the center of the crimson runner and strides forward, hands tucked casually into the pockets of his loose trousers. The ambassador blanches, beads of sweat breaking out across his forehead as his bluster evaporates.

"What is the matter, Ambassador?" Eiden asks, a lazy smirk tugging at his lips. "Won't you kneel before your Creator?"

}
