choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Creator, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Creator)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Creator
TEXT:

The hall plunges into absolute silence. The ambassador's face drains of color as he bends into a deep, trembling bow before the newcomer.

"I remain your humble servant," the ambassador chokes out, every word sounding like glass in his throat. "It is an honor to receive you in these halls, O Great Creator."

"What?!" Daren and Flower shout in unison.

"Hold on," Hadrik rumbles, eyes wide. "Creator as in the founder of Varathian civilization? That Creator?"

"I am surprised this is news to you," Eiden says, tilting his head with mild amusement. "Did Arraka fail to mention it? She was present when the first stones were laid, after all."

A beat of stunned silence falls over our group. Every eye turns toward the amulet at Hadrik's hip.

"What, did that slip my mind?" Arraka cackles from the metal casing. "How terribly careless of me! AHAHAHAHA!"

"So you are the Creator I have heard so much about," Golmyck says, stroking his chin from the throne.

"Indeed I am!" Eiden beams. "And I must say, King of Thilias, I am a great admirer of your work. Stripping this palace of every jewel and gold candlestick, auctioning the royal treasury, and distributing the coin to the poor—the continent's nobility wept in outrage for months. A delightfully entertaining spectacle."

"The palace belongs to the people, not the crown," Golmyck replies calmly. "I did only what any true representative would do in my seat."

"I fear you credit your subjects with virtues they do not possess," Eiden says with a pleasant smile.

"Creator," the ambassador interjects, struggling to regain his footing. "Your word is supreme across the continent of Varathia, but I must protest—"

"'The continent of Varathia...'" Eiden cuts him off, his voice laced with mocking wonder. "You mortals cling to that title so desperately. In truth, Varathia is a speck compared to the great continents across the sea. An island, nothing more. That is how I christened this land after the Still Winter—the Island of Varathia, an isle of freedom. Why inflate it into a continent? Did the discovery of the outside world bruise your collective vanity? Did you need a grander name to convince yourselves you rivaled the empires across the ocean?"

"Creator, please," the ambassador pleads, sweat dripping from his chin. "Whatever your interest in these travelers, blood has been shed. A royal life was extinguished. It would be a grave injustice if the earl's killers walked free."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Creator2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Creator2)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Creator2
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_slave == 3 || v_ch11_kill_noble != 0) {
"Oh?" Eiden's smile sharpens into something razor-edged. "And what if I told you I gave them the power to strike down Varathians in the first place?"

The ambassador turns pale, his mouth opening and closing without a sound. Eiden lets out a soft, delighted laugh.

"Do not look so stricken, Ambassador. I never suggested granting them unconditional pardon. But surely summary execution is a dreadfully dull end for mages of their talent."

"What do you... propose, then?" the ambassador asks, his voice barely a whisper.

"The arena, of course!" Eiden spreads his hands. "Throw them into the trials. If they survive to the end, it will signify the gods have seen fit to pardon their crimes. Spells are strictly outlawed on the sand, so they hold no magical advantage over the other gladiators. Suppression wards line the perimeter to ensure no cheating."

"Yes, but..." The ambassador hesitates. "If they somehow survive, the King of Ollendor will—"

"Are you suggesting the fabled gladiatorial arenas of Varathia—the crowning glory of your 'continent'—cannot handle a handful of mages stripped of their magic?" Eiden's smile widens, cruel and inviting.

"No..." the ambassador mutters, backing down. "No, of course not."

}
#if(v_ch11_kill_noble == 0 && v_ch11_saved_slave != 3) {
"Oh?" Eiden's smile sharpens into something razor-edged. "And what if I told you that I granted them the power to harm Varathians, and that I was the one who ended your precious earl's life?"

The ambassador turns pale, his mouth opening and closing without a sound. Eiden lets out a soft, delighted laugh.

"Do not look so stricken, Ambassador. I never suggested granting them unconditional pardon. But surely summary execution is a dreadfully dull end for mages of their talent."

"What do you... propose, then?" the ambassador asks, his voice barely a whisper.

"The arena, of course!" Eiden spreads his hands. "Throw them into the trials. If they survive to the end, it will signify the gods have seen fit to pardon their crimes. Spells are strictly outlawed on the sand, so they hold no magical advantage over the other gladiators. Suppression wards line the perimeter to ensure no cheating."

"Yes, but..." The ambassador hesitates. "If they somehow survive, the King of Ollendor will—"

"Are you suggesting the fabled gladiatorial arenas of Varathia—the crowning glory of your 'continent'—cannot handle a handful of mages stripped of their magic?" Eiden's smile widens, cruel and inviting.

"No..." the ambassador mutters, backing down. "No, of course not."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Creator3, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Creator3)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Creator3
TEXT:

The ambassador takes a moment to steady his breathing.

"Very well," he says at last. "The criminals will face judgment in the arena. But I will oversee the trials personally to ensure no interference."

"The arena is at your disposal, Ambassador," Golmyck agrees with an easy nod.

"Three days," the ambassador declares, finding his footing again. "In three days, I will stage the greatest spectacle in the history of Varathia. Until then, these prisoners will be confined to the holding cells beneath the arena alongside the regular gladiators."

"Then the matter is settled!" Eiden says brightly. "I eagerly await your spectacle, Ambassador. I will attend the main event in person. Do not disappoint me."

Without another word, the stillwater vanishes into thin air, leaving not a trace of residual aura behind.

"You criminals come with me," the ambassador orders, turning toward us. "I will not have you wandering the avenues endangering citizens."

"One moment, Ambassador," Golmyck cuts in, raising a small hand. "I require a private audience with our guests first. My retainers will deliver them to the holding cells shortly."

"Hmph." The ambassador scowls. "Do as you wish. I have preparations to make. Ensure they are locked beneath the arena before midnight."

He sweeps past us and storms out of the hall, the heavy oak doors slamming in his wake.

"Well then," Golmyck says, hopping down from the throne. "Follow me. I have no desire to hold our council in this drafty hall. Let me show you my real workshop."

choice(""What makes you think that we'll simply agree to be led to the arena?"", B2-Ch01a-Agree, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Agree)
choice(""Real castle? How many castles do you have in this place, anyway?"", B2-Ch01a-Castles, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Castles)
choice(""So, what exactly are these events that we need to win in order be set free, then?"", B2-Ch01a-Events, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Events)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Agree
TEXT:

"What makes you think that we'll simply agree to be led to the arena?"

"You are free to resist, of course," Golmyck says mildly. "Though doing so carries immediate disqualification from the tournament. Is that your intention?"

I open my mouth to counter, but no useful retort comes to mind. I keep quiet.

"I thought as much," Golmyck says. "Whatever drives you to risk your lives in this contest, you will not let a detour in the arena turn you aside. Consider it a preliminary trial to sharpen your blades. Now, where did I put that release?... Ah, here."

The gnome reaches behind the masonry of the throne and pulls a concealed iron lever. A section of the stone floor grinds back on hidden rollers, revealing a narrow stair descending into the foundations.

"Barry, look after the hall until I return," Golmyck calls out.

"What?..." I say, blinking.

"Understood, Your Highness," the servant replies from the dais.

"My apologies," Golmyck chuckles. "I was addressing my chamberlain. He shares your name."

He gestures toward the opening.

"Down here, if you please."

We trade glances and follow the gnome down the stone steps. The stair opens into a sprawling subterranean workshop cluttered with copper piping, gear assemblies, half-finished automatons, and discarded tools strewn across the floor. In the center sits a broad workbench dominated by an oversized brass transmitter, its base glowing with a steady green rune.

Golmyck strips off his mantle and circlet, hanging them on a wooden peg, and slips into the grease-stained lab coat we saw during the broadcast. Looking around the room, I recognize the backdrop immediately—this is where he transmitted the opening briefing, and the brass machine on the bench is what he was repairing.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Shapeshift, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Shapeshift)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Castles
TEXT:

"Real castle? How many castles do you have in this place, anyway?"

"Not in the literal sense," Golmyck chuckles. "You will see what I mean shortly. Now, where did I put that release?... Ah, here."

The gnome reaches behind the masonry of the throne and pulls a concealed iron lever. A section of the stone floor grinds back on hidden rollers, revealing a narrow stair descending into the foundations.

