Golmyck strips off his mantle and circlet, hanging them on a wooden peg, and slips into the grease-stained lab coat we saw during the broadcast. Looking around the room, I recognize the backdrop immediately—this is where he transmitted the opening briefing, and the brass machine on the bench is what he was repairing.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Shapeshift, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Shapeshift)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Events
TEXT:

"So, what exactly are these events that we need to win in order be set free, then?"

"Combat against gladiators, wild beasts, and assorted lethal contrivances," Golmyck replies. "Beyond that, the format shifts with every tournament. Given that the ambassador is staging this spectacle, expect the rules to be engineered entirely for your demise. Now, where did I put that release?... Ah, here."

The gnome reaches behind the masonry of the throne and pulls a concealed iron lever. A section of the stone floor grinds back on hidden rollers, revealing a narrow stair descending into the foundations.

"Barry, look after the hall until I return," Golmyck calls out.

"What?..." I say, blinking.

"Understood, Your Highness," the servant replies from the dais.

"My apologies," Golmyck chuckles. "I was addressing my chamberlain. He shares your name."

He gestures toward the opening.

"Down here, if you please."

We trade glances and follow the gnome down the stone steps. The stair opens into a sprawling subterranean workshop cluttered with copper piping, gear assemblies, half-finished automatons, and discarded tools strewn across the floor. In the center sits a broad workbench dominated by an oversized brass transmitter, its base glowing with a steady green rune.

Golmyck strips off his mantle and circlet, hanging them on a wooden peg, and slips into the grease-stained lab coat we saw during the broadcast. Looking around the room, I recognize the backdrop immediately—this is where he transmitted the opening briefing, and the brass machine on the bench is what he was repairing.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Shapeshift, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Shapeshift)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Shapeshift
TEXT:

"Welcome to my sanctum within the palace," Golmyck says, wiping a smudge of engine grease from a bronze casing. "I discovered this vault shortly after taking the crown and converted the space into a proper workshop. Affairs of state leave me far too little time for engineering, but an hour down here clears the head after blustering fools like the ambassador."

"You mentioned a private matter?" Daren asks, resting a hand on his pommel.

"I did," Golmyck says, turning to face us. "Let us begin with our giant friend, currently masquerading as a dwarf."

"Ah, blast it all," Hadrik groans, his shoulders dropping. "I knew the luck wouldn't hold. Is this where you banish me from the contest?"

"Not in the slightest," Golmyck replies with a grin. "The tournament charter contains no explicit ban against giants. We simply declined to issue invitations to your clans because your natural mass and strength skew the competition unfairly. However, since you took it upon yourself to compress your frame into dwarven proportions, we are prepared to grant an exception. You may compete—provided you never expand beyond the dimensions of a standard dwarf. Furthermore, you will maintain this dwarven persona before every competitor. No one in the arena may learn your true heritage. Are we agreed?"

"Agreed and settled!" Hadrik's booming laugh rattles the glassware on the bench.

"In that case, you are dismissed," Golmyck says. "I have no further business with you."

"Dismissed to the cells, you mean?"

"Dismissed to pursue your pinecones," Golmyck corrects. "You were not present during the altercation with the noble. You committed no crime on our streets, so we have no legal cause to detain you. You need not enter the arena."

"The hell I won't!" Hadrik barks, planting his fists on his hips. "Did I cross an ocean to forage in the pine needles? I came for blood and glory! If you won't throw me into the sand with my friends, I'll smash up the town square until your guards drag me down in chains!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Apology, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Apology)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Apology
TEXT:

"If you insist on volunteering for confinement," Golmyck chuckles, shaking his head, "I can draft a royal letter of admission for the arena marshals. The royal seal will open the gates."

"Much obliged!" Hadrik grins broadly.

"Shall I draft a similar pass for the young lady and her companion?" Golmyck asks, looking toward Flower.

"Uh, well—" Flower starts, but Illuna takes the floor, Flower's eyes turning brilliant blue.

"No," Illuna says in a cool, level tone. "We will observe from the spectator gallery."

"Very well," Golmyck nods. "I will provide a gallery pass."

"You did your homework," Hadrik notes, leaning against a worktable. "I didn't think your scouts would spot Illuna."

"My watchers pay close attention to foreign casters," Golmyck says. "They have tracked your movements since you entered the outer gates."

"Then they saw what happened with the noble?" Daren asks quietly.

"They did," Golmyck's expression sombers. "Which brings me to the second reason I brought you down here. First, I owe you an apology. The spectacle you encountered in our market must have sickened men and women of conscience."

"You sound as though you despise the practice," Kate says, her arms crossed.

"I do," Golmyck replies, his voice hardening. "Treating sentient beings as chattel is a blight upon this land, and every citizen shares the guilt of its endurance. I am little better than the kings before me; I have made almost no progress in dismantling the trade since taking this seat."

"Why?" Daren presses, stepping closer to the workbench. "You wear the crown. You command the city. You hold the power to end it."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Interfere, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Interfere)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Interfere
TEXT:

"Look above you," Golmyck says, pointing a wrench toward a complex brass apparatus suspended from the vaulted ceiling.

"What of it?" Daren asks, glancing upward.

"A dampening field of my own design," the gnome explains. "Its sole function is to suppress acoustic and magical transmission beyond these walls. I mounted it the first week I took residence."

"You suspect someone is listening?" I ask.

"I know they are," Golmyck replies grimly. "The upper chambers are riddled with listening relays. I am not the only authority wielding an intelligence network in Thilias. The crown's actual control over this city is far more precarious than appearances suggest."

"Who keeps you under surveillance?" Hadrik asks. "The other monarchs?"

"In a manner of speaking," Golmyck says. "A lessathi enclave operates within our walls. They maintain alliances with the other regional kings while pursuing their own agenda. Their network of informants and assassins rivals my own, and their mandate is to ensure no ruler takes initiatives that threaten their vision for Varathia. If I signed an emancipation decree tomorrow, an assassin's needle would find my neck before the wax cooled."

"So you surrender the field?" Daren asks, his voice tight.

"Hardly," Golmyck counters. "I have spent years brokering a secret pact with the animal clans. Monster incursions have steadily shrunk their forest reserves, making their elders far more pragmatic of late. I offered them territory and sanctuary within our walls in exchange for their warriors helping us cleanse the lessathi presence from Thilias. An envoy arrives in this very chamber in three days to finalize terms. If the talks hold, we strike within the season."

"So you have matters in hand," Hadrik says, "and we should keep our noses clean?"

"Precisely," Golmyck says. "I wanted you to understand the balance before you staged another well-intentioned riot in my streets."

choice(""Actually, I think we've already filled our quota of rash actions for the week."", B2-Ch01a-Documents, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Documents, v_b2_ch1_interfere = 0)
choice(""A few months is too long. Maybe we can take care of your lessathi problem, instead."", B2-Ch01a-Documents, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Documents, v_b2_ch1_interfere = 1)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Documents
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch1_interfere == 0) {
"Actually, I think we've already filled our quota of rash actions for the week," I say.

"These lessathi you spoke of..." Kate steps forward, her gaze intense. "Do they employ artificial mages? Is there an elementalist named Diane—a woman who commands lightning—working in their ranks?"

"Not to my knowledge," Golmyck replies. "My scouts would have reported any artificial casters operating in Thilias."

"I see..." Kate looks away, her jaw tight.

"One final matter before you depart," Golmyck says, addressing the room. "If you survive the arena trials and earn your acquittal, return to this workshop. I will issue royal charters confirming your sentence was served. You will not need them within Thilias, but marshals in other domains will demand proof that the Earl of Ollendor's death was answered. Now, let me draft the dwarf's admission writ so you can be on your way."

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_interfere == 1) {
"A few months is too long. Maybe we can take care of your lessathi problem, instead."

"Indeed?" Golmyck cocks an eyebrow, stroking his chin. "Do you speak on behalf of your entire company, young lessathi, or only for yourself?"

The question catches me flat-footed. We have traveled together for a week, but committing the entire group to storming a fortified lessathi enclave is a heavy bet to make on my own.

Before the hesitation can show, Daren steps up beside me, resting a heavy gauntlet on my shoulder.

"He speaks for me as well," Daren tells the king.

"And me," Hadrik rumbles, stepping forward with a grim smile. "After seeing what that noble did in the street yesterday, I'd gladly crack the skulls of the bastards running this racket."

"Fascinating," Golmyck turns to Kate. "And what of our artificial mage? Do you volunteer for this raid as well?"

"These lessathi you spoke of..." Kate says, her eyes narrowing. "Do they employ other artificial mages? Is there an elementalist named Diane, who commands lightning, among them?"

"Not to my knowledge," Golmyck answers. "My scouts would have flagged any artificial casters operating within the walls."

"I see..." Kate pauses, her fingers tightening at her side. "Even if Diane is not here, their archives may hold records of her location. If you intend to breach their stronghold, I am coming."

"If everyone is going, we're going too, right Petal?" Flower asks with bright enthusiasm.

Flower's eyes shift to cold blue as Illuna lets out a long, weary sigh, though she offers no objection.

"I must admit," Golmyck smiles, genuinely impressed, "I did not expect such unconditional resolve."

He pauses, gathering his thoughts.

"Very well. I will weigh your offer. We will revisit the strategy once you emerge from the arena. When you secure your freedom, return to this workshop. I will have royal charters ready confirming your sentence was served, protecting you from arrest in foreign cities for the earl's death. Now, let me draft the dwarf's admission writ so you can be on your way."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch01a-Ending, v_current_scene = B2-Ch01a-Ending)


ID: B2-Ch01a-Ending
TEXT:

Golmyck scratches a few lines on parchment, presses his signet ring into warm red wax, and hands the scroll to Hadrik. As we turn toward the stairs, the gnome raises a finger.

"One final warning," Golmyck says, his voice dead serious. "Do not speak a single syllable of what we discussed here while you are within this city. Not even in private whispers. Walls carry echoes, and eavesdroppers lurk everywhere. I brought you into this vault because I judged you trustworthy. Do not prove me wrong."

"You have our word," Daren replies firmly. "We will be discreet."

"Splendid. May the gods walk with you in the sand."

"May they watch over us all," Daren answers.

We bid the gnome farewell and climb the winding stone stairs back to the upper hall. The royal chamberlain waits beside the open threshold on the dais.

"Follow me, if you please," the servant says, leading the way toward the main gates. "Stay close so you do not wander."

"Don't worry, Barry, we're right behind you!" Hadrik calls out, then turns to give me an enormous, shit-eating grin. "Isn't that right, Barry?"

"Shut up, Hadrik," I mutter as we follow the servant through the arched gateway and out toward our new prison.

